        "heading": "What 'antioxidant' actually means",
        "body": "Your body constantly produces reactive oxygen species (free radicals) as a normal by-product of using oxygen for energy. In balance, they are useful signalling molecules. When they outpace your defences — a state called oxidative stress — they can damage lipids, proteins and DNA over time.\n\nAn antioxidant is simply a molecule that helps neutralise excess free radicals or supports the body's own defence systems (like glutathione and various enzymes). It's a mechanism, not a magic switch — and 'antioxidant in a test tube' does not automatically mean 'meaningful benefit in a living person.'"
