    "metaDesc": "The real side-effect profile of purified Shilajit, heavy-metal risks of raw Shilajit, and who should avoid it.",
    "keyword": "shilajit side effects",
    "intent": "Informational",
    "pillar": "Safety",
    "summary": "A doctor's honest look at Shilajit side effects, why purification matters, and who should be cautious before starting.",
    "readTime": "7 min read",
    "intro": "Shilajit is one of Ayurveda's most celebrated rasayanas (rejuvenatives), and interest in it has grown fast. But \"natural\" does not automatically mean \"risk-free\". The most important safety fact about Shilajit is simple: the quality and processing of the material matter far more than the herb-mineral itself.\n\nIn this article I'll walk through the realistic side-effect profile of properly purified (shodhit) Shilajit, the genuine dangers of raw or poorly sourced material, and the groups of people who are better off avoiding it or checking with a physician first. My aim is to help you make an informed decision, not to alarm or oversell.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What Shilajit Actually Is",
        "body": "Shilajit is a sticky, tar-like exudate that seeps from rocks in high-altitude regions, formed over centuries from decomposed plant matter. Its studied active constituents include fulvic and humic acids, dibenzo-alpha-pyrones and various trace minerals.\n\nIn classical Ayurveda, Shilajit is never used in its raw state. The texts insist on shodhana — a purification process — before it is considered fit for internal use. This traditional caution turns out to line up neatly with modern safety concerns, which we'll come to below."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Side Effects of Purified Shilajit",
        "body": "When properly purified and used sensibly, Shilajit is generally well tolerated. Reported side effects tend to be mild and uncommon: some people notice a warm or \"heaty\" feeling, mild digestive upset, changes in appetite, or a headache, especially when they start with too much at once.\n\nBecause Shilajit is considered ushna (heating) in nature, individuals with a strong pitta constitution may be more prone to acidity, restlessness or skin flare-ups. It's worth being honest here: the modern clinical evidence base for Shilajit is still limited and mostly small-scale, so the full long-term side-effect picture in humans is not comprehensively mapped. Starting low and observing how your body responds is sensible."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The Real Risk: Raw and Adulterated Shilajit",
        "body": "The most serious safety issue with Shilajit has little to do with the substance itself and everything to do with contamination. Raw, unprocessed Shilajit can carry heavy metals such as lead, arsenic and mercury, along with free radicals, fungal contamination and mycotoxins. Consuming raw \"straight from the mountain\" material is genuinely risky.\n\nThis is exactly why shodhana and modern testing exist. Reputable products should be purified and independently lab-tested for heavy metals and microbial load, ideally batch by batch. If a seller cannot show you third-party heavy-metal reports, treat that as a red flag — the cheapest, unverified Shilajit is often the most dangerous."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Interactions and Medication Concerns",
        "body": "Shilajit may influence blood pressure, blood sugar and iron levels, so it can theoretically interact with medicines used for these conditions. Because it can add to your iron intake, people with iron-overload conditions such as haemochromatosis or thalassemia should be particularly careful.\n\nIf you take regular medication — for diabetes, blood pressure, thyroid, blood thinning or anything for a chronic illness — please speak to a qualified physician before adding Shilajit. This isn't a formality; it's the responsible way to avoid unwanted overlaps."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who Should Avoid Shilajit",
        "body": "Some groups should simply not use Shilajit without medical guidance. Pregnant and breastfeeding women should avoid it, as safety in these situations has not been established. Children should not be given Shilajit unless a doctor specifically advises it.\n\nOthers who should be cautious include people with active gout or high uric acid, those with iron-overload disorders, anyone with significant kidney or heart conditions, and people scheduled for surgery. When in doubt, the safe default is to check with an Ayurvedic or medical professional first."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How to Use It Responsibly",
        "body": "If you do choose to use Shilajit, choose quality over price. Look for genuinely purified resin, transparency about sourcing, and current third-party heavy-metal and microbial test reports. Avoid loose, unbranded lumps sold with dramatic claims.\n\nStart with a small amount, take it with warm water or milk rather than on a very empty stomach if it feels heaty, and pay attention to how you feel over the first couple of weeks. Consistency and moderation matter more than large amounts — more is not better here."
      }
    ],
