    "intro": "Ayurveda holds a simple but powerful idea: your health is built less by occasional big efforts and more by what you do every single day. That daily rhythm is called *dinacharya* (from *dina*, day, and *acharya*, conduct or regimen). It's a sequence of gentle practices — waking at a consistent hour, caring for your mouth and skin, moving your body, eating at regular times, and winding down for sleep — designed to keep your doshas steady and your digestion strong.\n\nThe beauty of dinacharya is that it doesn't demand a monastery or a free morning. It asks for consistency, not perfection. Below is a practical, modern-friendly walk through a classical day, with honest notes on where tradition and current evidence line up — and where the science is still catching up.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why a routine matters in Ayurveda",
        "body": "Ayurveda maps the day onto the three doshas, with each holding sway at certain hours — Kapha in the early morning and evening, Pitta at midday and midnight, Vata in the late afternoon and before dawn. Living in step with these cycles is thought to support *agni* (digestive fire), steady energy and clearer sleep. When we ignore them — eating late, sleeping at random hours, staying wired past midnight — Ayurveda sees this as a slow accumulation of imbalance.\n\nModern chronobiology echoes part of this wisdom: our circadian rhythm genuinely influences digestion, hormones, alertness and sleep quality, and irregular schedules are linked with poorer metabolic health. Ayurveda's dosha framework is a traditional model rather than a proven biological clock, but the shared conclusion is practical and sound — regular timing helps the body run well."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Waking and morning cleansing",
        "body": "Classical texts suggest waking in the *Brahma muhurta* — roughly the pre-dawn window before sunrise — as a calm, Vata-governed time ideal for a gentle start. If pre-dawn isn't realistic, aim simply for a consistent wake time. Begin by rehydrating, sometimes with warm water, and attending to natural elimination, which Ayurveda values as a sign of healthy digestion.\n\nOral care comes next: brushing, gently scraping the tongue with a copper or steel scraper to clear overnight coating, and *gandusha/kavala* (oil pulling), traditionally with sesame or coconut oil. Tongue scraping plausibly reduces coating and freshens the mouth. Evidence for oil pulling is limited and mixed — it may modestly support oral hygiene but is not a replacement for brushing, flossing or dental care."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Abhyanga: self-massage with oil",
        "body": "*Abhyanga*, a warm-oil self-massage before bathing, is one of dinacharya's most loved practices. Sesame oil is common for Vata, coconut for Pitta-type heat; the strokes are unhurried, moving along the limbs and joints. Traditionally it is credited with nourishing the skin, easing stiffness, calming the nervous system and promoting sound sleep.\n\nDirect clinical research on daily Abhyanga is modest, though many people find it deeply relaxing and skin-softening — and gentle massage more broadly is associated with reduced tension. Keep it simple: a few minutes on a busy day still counts. Avoid oiling over broken or inflamed skin, and skip vigorous massage if you're unwell or pregnant without professional guidance."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Meals, movement and midday",
        "body": "Ayurveda treats the midday meal — when Pitta and digestive fire peak — as the day's largest and most important. The guidance is timeless: eat at regular hours, sit down and eat mindfully, favour warm freshly-cooked food, and avoid grazing before the previous meal has digested. Light movement or yoga in the morning, and a short walk after meals, supports both digestion and steadiness.\n\nThis aligns comfortably with modern nutrition: consistent meal timing, front-loading calories earlier in the day, and post-meal walking all have supportive evidence for metabolism and blood-sugar response. You don't need an elaborate diet — regularity and attention do a lot of the work."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Evening wind-down and sleep",
        "body": "As Kapha rises again in the evening, Ayurveda advises a lighter, earlier dinner and a gradual slowing down. A calming ritual — dim lights, a warm bath, gentle oil on the feet or scalp, reduced screens — signals the body toward rest. Sleeping before late night is emphasised, since the hours around midnight are considered restorative.\n\nSleep science strongly agrees here: consistent bedtimes, lower light and screen exposure at night, and a lighter evening meal all improve sleep quality. This is one area where classical and modern advice overlap almost perfectly."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where products and supplements fit",
        "body": "Dinacharya is fundamentally about habits, not products — timing, food, movement and rest come first. Some people choose to add traditional *rasayana* (rejuvenative) preparations, such as purified Shilajit, as part of a morning routine. If you do, treat it as a small supporting piece within a well-built foundation, not a shortcut around it.\n\nQuality and appropriateness matter more than any single ingredient. Choose lab-tested products, follow label guidance, and — importantly — consult a qualified physician before starting any supplement if you are pregnant, breastfeeding, on medication, or managing a chronic condition."
      }
    ],
