    "readTime": "7 min read",
    "intro": "In Ayurveda, Vata is the dosha of movement — the subtle force behind every breath, heartbeat, nerve impulse and thought. Made of the elements air (vayu) and ether (akasha), Vata governs circulation, elimination, communication between cells, and the quick play of the mind.\n\nWhen Vata is in balance, you feel light, creative, energetic and adaptable. When it goes out of balance, that same lightness can tip into dryness, anxiety, restlessness and fatigue. This guide walks you through Vata's traits, how to spot aggravation early, and the practical food, lifestyle and herbal choices that classical Ayurveda uses to steady it. Think of it as education, not a prescription — your own constitution is best read by a qualified physician.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What Vata Dosha Is (and Its Core Qualities)",
        "body": "Vata is described in the classical texts through a set of qualities (gunas): dry (ruksha), light (laghu), cold (shita), rough (khara), subtle (sukshma), mobile (chala) and clear (vishada). Understanding these qualities is the whole key — because balance in Ayurveda works on the principle that 'like increases like, and opposites reduce.'\n\nSo anything dry, cold, light and irregular tends to raise Vata, while warm, moist, grounding and routine-based inputs tend to calm it. Vata is naturally highest in the later stages of life, in the monsoon and early winter, and in the pre-dawn and late-afternoon hours — useful to know when you plan meals and rest."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Recognising a Vata Prakriti (Constitution)",
        "body": "People with a Vata-dominant prakriti often have a lighter, thinner frame, find it hard to gain weight, and have dry skin or hair and cold hands and feet. They tend to be quick thinkers, imaginative, talkative and enthusiastic — great at starting things, sometimes less consistent at finishing them.\n\nDigestion in Vata types is often variable — good one day, bloated or irregular the next. Sleep can be light and easily disturbed. Remember, most of us are a blend of two doshas; a pure single-dosha constitution is uncommon. Your prakriti is fixed at birth, while your current imbalance (vikriti) is what changes and what you actually work on."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Signs Vata Has Become Aggravated",
        "body": "Common early signals of raised Vata include dry skin and lips, constipation or hard stools, gas and bloating, cracking joints, and feeling cold. On the mental side, aggravated Vata can show up as anxiety, racing or scattered thoughts, restlessness, and disturbed, light sleep.\n\nLifestyle triggers are very familiar to modern life: irregular meal and sleep times, skipping meals, excessive travel, too much screen time, over-exercise, cold and raw foods, and chronic stress. These are educational patterns to notice — persistent or severe symptoms always deserve proper medical assessment rather than self-diagnosis."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Foods That Steady Vata",
        "body": "To counter Vata's dry, cold and light nature, favour warm, moist, well-cooked and mildly oily foods with a naturally grounding quality. Cooked grains like rice and wheat, moong dal, warming soups and stews, ghee, warm milk, ripe sweet fruits, and cooked seasonal vegetables are classically recommended. The sweet, sour and salty tastes are considered pacifying for Vata.\n\nIt helps to eat at regular times, sit down calmly for meals, and favour warm water over iced drinks. Ayurveda suggests going easy on very dry, raw, cold, or excessively bitter and astringent foods (large amounts of raw salads, dry crackers, or an over-reliance on caffeine), which can nudge Vata higher — especially in cold, windy weather."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Daily Routine and Lifestyle",
        "body": "Vata thrives on rhythm. A steady dinacharya (daily routine) — consistent wake, meal and sleep times — is arguably the single most powerful tool for Vata people. Warm oil self-massage (abhyanga) with sesame oil, keeping warm, and grounding practices like slow yoga, gentle walking and pranayama all help settle the nervous, mobile quality of Vata.\n\nAim for adequate, early rest; the pre-midnight hours matter. Avoid overstimulation late at night and cut back on excessive multitasking. Where movement is concerned, choose steady, moderate exercise over erratic high-intensity bursts. These are supportive habits, not medical treatment."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Herbs Traditionally Used for Vata",
        "body": "Classical Ayurveda associates several rasayana (rejuvenating) and grounding herbs with Vata support — warming, nourishing or unctuous substances such as ashwagandha, bala, dashamoola, and ghee-based preparations, along with digestive spices like ginger, cumin, ajwain and asafoetida used gently in cooking.\n\nShilajit is also classically valued as a rasayana. It is important to be honest here: while these have a long traditional history and some early modern research, robust large-scale clinical evidence for many specific claims is still limited or preliminary. Herbs are not a substitute for medical care, and quality and purification matter a great deal — especially with mineral-based substances like Shilajit, which should be properly purified (shodhit). Always choose tested products and consult a qualified Ayurvedic physician before starting anything, particularly in pregnancy, chronic illness, or if you take regular medication."
      }
    ],
