    "readTime": "7 min read",
    "intro": "If you have ever wondered why Ayurveda speaks of doshas, elements, gunas and the \"mind\" as though they belong to one connected map, the answer often lies in an older system of thought: Samkhya (Sankhya) philosophy. Long before Ayurveda organised itself into a science of health, Samkhya offered Indian thinkers a way of explaining how the entire universe — from raw matter to the subtle workings of the mind — unfolds from a few basic principles.\n\nAyurveda borrowed this framework and put it to practical use. This article walks through the core ideas of Samkhya and shows how they quietly underpin much of what Ayurveda says about the body, mind and the natural world. It is educational in intent, not a guide to treatment.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What is Samkhya philosophy?",
        "body": "Samkhya is one of the six classical schools (darshanas) of Indian philosophy, traditionally attributed to the sage Kapila. Its name comes from a root meaning \"to enumerate\" or \"to count\" — fitting, because Samkhya is essentially a careful counting-out of the building blocks of reality. It asks a simple but profound question: how does the whole of experience, mental and physical, arise?\n\nSamkhya answers with a dualism of two eternal realities: Purusha (pure consciousness, the silent witness) and Prakriti (primordial nature, the active, creative substance). Everything we can observe — thoughts, senses, bodies, matter — is considered an evolution of Prakriti, while Purusha simply witnesses it. This elegant model gave Indian medicine a ready-made language for describing how a person is more than just a physical body."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Purusha and Prakriti: consciousness and nature",
        "body": "In Samkhya, Purusha is unchanging awareness — it does not act, it only observes. Prakriti is the opposite: it is unconscious but endlessly active, the source of all form and movement. Creation, in this view, begins when the presence of Purusha \"disturbs\" the equilibrium of Prakriti, setting evolution in motion.\n\nAyurveda finds this distinction useful. It reminds practitioners that a human being is a union of a conscious presence and a natural, material body-mind that constantly changes. Health, then, is not only physical balance but also the harmony between our changing nature and the steady awareness that observes it — an idea that flows into Ayurvedic ethics, lifestyle (sadvritta) and mental wellbeing."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The three gunas: sattva, rajas and tamas",
        "body": "Prakriti is said to be made of three qualities or gunas: sattva (clarity, harmony, lightness), rajas (activity, movement, restlessness) and tamas (inertia, heaviness, dullness). Before creation these three sit in perfect balance; creation unfolds when the balance tips. Every object and every state of mind is a particular blend of these three.\n\nAyurveda uses the gunas mainly to describe the mind. A predominantly sattvic mind is considered calm and discerning; rajasic tendencies bring drive but also agitation; tamasic states bring lethargy and confusion. Classical texts describe mental constitutions in these terms, which is why Ayurvedic guidance on diet, routine and conduct often aims to nourish sattva. It is worth being honest here: these are traditional psychological categories, not clinically measured parameters."
      },
      {
        "heading": "From subtle to gross: the twenty-four principles",
        "body": "Samkhya maps a stepwise evolution from Prakriti. First arises Mahat or Buddhi (intellect), then Ahamkara (the sense of \"I\"), then the mind (manas), the five sense faculties, the five faculties of action, the five subtle elements (tanmatras) and finally the five gross elements (mahabhutas): space, air, fire, water and earth. Together these make up the classical twenty-four principles (tattvas).\n\nThis is arguably Samkhya's biggest gift to Ayurveda. The five mahabhutas are the raw material for the Panchamahabhuta theory, and the doshas — Vata, Pitta and Kapha — are understood as expressions of these elements in the body. So when Ayurveda describes a food, herb or season by its qualities, it is drawing on a chain of reasoning that begins in Samkhya cosmology."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Why this matters for how Ayurveda sees health",
        "body": "Because body, mind and cosmos share the same elemental grammar, Ayurveda can reason across them: what happens in nature (seasons, foods, times of day) is seen to affect the elements within us. This is the philosophical basis for Ayurveda's emphasis on aligning daily and seasonal routines with the natural world.\n\nIt also explains Ayurveda's holistic outlook. Treating a person means considering their physical constitution (prakriti), mental temperament (guna balance) and their relationship with awareness itself. Modern science studies these dimensions separately and with different tools; Samkhya offered an integrated, if pre-modern, model. Appreciating it helps us read Ayurvedic texts on their own terms rather than expecting them to speak the language of a laboratory."
      }
    ],
