        "heading": "Why purified matters more than the pincode",
        "body": "Raw Shilajit scraped from rocks can carry sand, soil, mycotoxins and, most importantly, heavy metals like lead, arsenic and mercury that concentrate naturally in some mineral environments. Classical Ayurveda never intended it to be consumed raw — the texts describe shodhana, a purification process (often using decoctions such as Triphala) to remove impurities before the resin is fit for use.\n\nThis is why a well-processed resin from an ordinary-sounding source can be far safer than an unpurified \"premium Kashmir\" lump. The transformation from rock exudate to usable Shilajit happens in the purification, not in the postcode. Judge the process, not the poetry of the name."
