    "slug": "shilajit-water-test",
    "title": "The Shilajit Water Test, Done Properly",
    "metaTitle": "The Shilajit Water Test: How to Do It (and Its Limits)",
    "metaDesc": "A step-by-step water-dissolution test for Shilajit, what results mean, and why it's not proof.",
    "keyword": "shilajit water test",
    "intent": "HowTo",
    "pillar": "Buying & Authenticity",
    "summary": "A practical, step-by-step guide to the Shilajit water test—how to do it correctly, how to read the results, and why it's a useful screen but never final proof of purity.",
    "readTime": "6 min read",
    "intro": "If you've bought Shilajit resin, you've probably seen the \"drop it in water\" test shared across social media. Done properly, it's a handy first check that can flag obvious fakes. Done carelessly, it can mislead you in both directions—labelling a good product as bad, or reassuring you about a poor one. This guide walks you through the water-dissolution test the way it should be done, explains what the results actually indicate, and is honest about its real limits. Think of it as one clue among several, not a verdict.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What the test is actually checking",
        "body": "Genuine purified (shodhit) Shilajit resin is a mineral-pitch preparation that is largely water-soluble. When you place a small amount in warm water, it should gradually dissolve into a golden-brown to reddish or coffee-coloured solution without leaving hard, gritty, or plastic-like residue.\n\nThe logic is simple: many common adulterants—soil, clay, chalk, gum, jaggery, or synthetic binders—behave differently in water. Some sink as sediment, some cloud the water, some turn it milky, and some refuse to dissolve at all. The test is really a rough solubility and residue check, not a chemical assay."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Step-by-step: doing it properly",
        "body": "Take a pea-sized amount (roughly 200-300 mg) of resin. Use a clean glass of lukewarm or room-temperature water—not boiling, which can distort results, and not icy cold, which slows dissolution. Drop the resin in and leave it undisturbed for a few minutes, then stir gently.\n\nGood-quality resin typically dissolves fairly completely, tinting the water a clear golden-brown to deep reddish-brown. Give it time; resin dissolves slower than powder. Observe three things: the colour of the water, whether it stays translucent or turns cloudy, and what (if anything) settles at the bottom."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How to read the results",
        "body": "A translucent golden to reddish-brown solution with little or no residue is an encouraging sign. Fine, dark particles that eventually dissolve are usually fine. What should concern you: a milky or chalky white cloudiness (possible chalk or starch), a thick muddy sediment that won't dissolve (possible soil or clay), a greasy film, or bits that float and clump like glue.\n\nColour alone isn't decisive—shade varies with concentration and water quality. The clearest red flags are heavy insoluble residue and unnatural cloudiness rather than a specific colour."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Why this is NOT proof of purity",
        "body": "Here is the honest part every buyer deserves: the water test can be fooled. A skilled adulterator can blend soluble fillers, gums, or humic-like substances that dissolve neatly and mimic a clean result. Passing the water test does not confirm your Shilajit is genuine, safe, or free from heavy metals.\n\nHeavy-metal contamination (lead, arsenic, mercury) is the most serious risk with unpurified or poorly sourced Shilajit—and it is completely invisible to a water test. No home test can detect it. This is a food-safety issue, not a folklore one, which is why sourcing and testing matter far more than any kitchen experiment."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The tests that actually matter",
        "body": "For real assurance, look for third-party laboratory testing: heavy-metal analysis (ideally to relevant Indian standards), and where available, markers such as fulvic acid content. Reputable brands publish or provide these reports on request. Transparency about origin, purification (shodhana) method, and batch testing tells you more than any viral video.\n\nAt 3TATTAVA, our RockResin Shodhit Shilajit is purified and lab-checked because we believe safety data, not stunts, should earn your trust. Treat the water test as a quick sanity check at home—and lab certification as the actual proof."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A sensible way to use the test",
        "body": "Use the water test as an early filter, not a final judge. If a product clouds the water milky white or leaves gritty sediment, that's a reasonable reason to be cautious and ask the seller questions. If it dissolves cleanly, treat that as 'no obvious red flag'—not as a guarantee.\n\nCombine it with the things that genuinely predict quality: a verifiable source, a clear purification process, and a current heavy-metal test report. If you have a health condition, take regular medication, or are pregnant or breastfeeding, speak to a qualified physician before using Shilajit at all."
      }
    ],
