    "summary": "The shilajit flame test is a handy at-home check, but it can be fooled — here's how to do it and why lab testing is what really matters.",
    "readTime": "6 min read",
    "intro": "If you've bought Shilajit resin online, you've probably seen someone hold a lump over a flame to \"prove\" it's real. It's a popular ritual — and it does tell you something useful. But here's the honest truth from someone who works with these products every day: the flame test is a screening tool, not a certificate of purity.\n\nIn this guide I'll walk you through how the test works, what a genuine resin tends to do, why some fakes pass anyway, and why a laboratory report is the only thing that can actually confirm what you're consuming.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What the flame test is meant to show",
        "body": "The idea is simple. Pure Shilajit resin is a mineral-rich, organic exudate — it is not a wax, a resin glue, or a sugar syrup. So when you apply a flame, a genuine resin is expected to behave in a characteristic way: it should not catch fire and burn like candle wax, and it typically bubbles or softens rather than igniting into a flame.\n\nPeople use the test as a quick way to rule out obvious adulterants like paraffin, jaggery, or chocolate-based fillers, which behave very differently when heated. Used this way, it's a reasonable first-pass check — nothing more."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How to do the flame test at home",
        "body": "Take a small pea-sized amount of resin on the tip of a metal spoon or a pin. Hold it over a lighter or candle flame for a few seconds and observe. A genuine resin generally does not catch alight; instead it may soften, bubble, or give off a faint earthy, smoky smell.\n\nA separate and often more telling check is the water test: a small amount of authentic resin usually dissolves in warm water to give a reddish-brown to golden solution, without leaving gritty sand or an oily film. Doing the flame and water observations together gives you more confidence than either alone."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Why some fakes still pass the flame test",
        "body": "This is the part most videos skip. The flame test only detects a narrow band of adulterants — the flammable ones. A clever counterfeit can be built from non-flammable, cheap materials such as clay, humic-acid powder, coffee, or mineral fillers mixed into a paste. Many of these will also refuse to burn, so they sail through the flame test looking perfectly 'genuine'.\n\nThe test also can't tell you about the things that matter most for safety: heavy metals like lead, arsenic and mercury, or contamination from poor processing. Raw Shilajit must be purified (shodhana) before use precisely because unprocessed material can carry these. A lump that passes the flame test can still be unpurified and unsafe."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Why lab testing is the real answer",
        "body": "A flame gives you a yes/no hunch. A laboratory gives you actual numbers. Third-party testing can measure fulvic acid content, screen for heavy metals against permissible limits, and check for microbial contamination — none of which your eyes or a lighter can do.\n\nAt 3TATTAVA our approach with RockResin is that authenticity should be provable on paper, not just performed for a camera. When you buy any Shilajit, ask the seller for a batch test report from an accredited lab. A brand confident in its sourcing will share one; that document tells you far more than any flame ever could."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How to buy Shilajit sensibly",
        "body": "Treat the flame and water tests as informal home checks, not proof. Combine them with the signals that are harder to fake: transparent sourcing, a physician or expert-led brand, clear labelling that says 'purified/shodhit', and — most importantly — accessible third-party lab reports.\n\nBe cautious of prices that seem too good to be true, dramatic 'burns like fire' or 'cures everything' marketing, and sellers who dodge questions about testing. Genuine Shilajit is a mineral-dense substance that takes real effort to source and purify, and that reality is reflected in how a serious brand talks about it."
      }
    ],
