    "readTime": "7 min read",
    "intro": "Ayurveda — literally the \"science (veda) of life (ayus)\" — is one of the world's oldest continuously practised systems of medicine. But its story is often either romanticised or oversimplified. In this article we trace where Ayurveda actually comes from, the texts and physicians who shaped it, how it travelled and evolved, and what it looks like now as a regulated discipline in modern India.\n\nUnderstanding this history matters. It helps us respect Ayurveda's genuine depth while staying honest about what it can and cannot claim in a modern clinical sense.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Roots in the Vedic period",
        "body": "Ayurveda's earliest ideas are traditionally traced to the Vedic era, with references to healing, herbs and health scattered through the Rigveda and especially the Atharvaveda. Ayurveda itself is often described as an *upaveda* — a body of applied knowledge linked to the Vedas rather than a Veda in its own right.\n\nAt this stage medicine was interwoven with ritual, philosophy and observation of nature. It is important to note that exact dates are debated by historians, and much early knowledge was transmitted orally for generations before being written down. So while the tradition is genuinely ancient, precise timelines should be read as approximate rather than settled fact."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The classical texts: the Brihat-trayi",
        "body": "Ayurveda took its mature, systematic form in a set of foundational Sanskrit treatises. The three most revered — known together as the *Brihat-trayi* (the \"Great Triad\") — are the Charaka Samhita, the Sushruta Samhita, and the Ashtanga Hridaya of Vagbhata.\n\nThe Charaka Samhita focuses on internal medicine (kaya-chikitsa), diagnosis, ethics and physician conduct. The Sushruta Samhita is remarkable for its detailed descriptions of surgery, instruments and procedures. Vagbhata's Ashtanga Hridaya, composed later, elegantly synthesises both. A later group of texts, the *Laghu-trayi* (Madhava Nidana, Sharngadhara Samhita and Bhavaprakasha), refined diagnosis, pharmacy and materia medica."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The scholars behind the system",
        "body": "Ayurveda is inseparable from the physicians who codified it. Charaka is associated with the school of internal medicine, and the text bearing his name is itself a revision of an earlier work attributed to Agnivesha. Sushruta, often called a pioneering figure in early surgery, is credited with techniques including approaches to wound care and reconstructive procedures.\n\nVagbhata brought clarity and structure that made Ayurveda teachable for centuries afterward. These were not solitary geniuses but representatives of living schools of thought (sampradayas) that debated, tested and passed on knowledge — a scholarly tradition of commentary and revision rather than fixed dogma."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Core principles that endured",
        "body": "Across these texts, certain organising ideas recur: the theory of the three doshas (vata, pitta, kapha), the concept of agni (digestive and metabolic fire), the seven dhatus (tissues), and an emphasis on prevention, diet, daily routine (dinacharya) and seasonal living (ritucharya).\n\nThese frameworks are best understood as a classical model for reasoning about health and constitution, not as modern biochemistry. They have real practical wisdom around lifestyle and prevention — but where specific therapeutic claims are made, they should be evaluated on their own modern evidence rather than accepted simply because they are old."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Travel, decline and revival",
        "body": "Ayurvedic knowledge did not stay within India's borders. Translations and exchanges reached the Islamic world, influencing and being influenced by other medical traditions, and echoes appear in Tibetan and Southeast Asian medicine. Within India, patronage shifted over centuries, and under colonial rule Ayurveda saw institutional decline as Western medicine was prioritised.\n\nThe late nineteenth and twentieth centuries brought a conscious revival, with new colleges, standardised curricula and organised practitioner bodies. This period reshaped Ayurveda from a largely guru-shishya oral tradition into a formally taught profession."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Ayurveda today: regulation under AYUSH",
        "body": "In modern India, Ayurveda is a recognised system of medicine governed within a formal framework. It sits under the Ministry of AYUSH (Ayurveda, Yoga & Naturopathy, Unani, Siddha and Homoeopathy), with education, practice and drug standards overseen by dedicated bodies and the relevant regulations for Ayurvedic, Siddha and Unani medicines.\n\nQualified practitioners today earn a BAMS degree, and there is growing effort — still uneven — to study Ayurvedic practices through modern research methods. The honest position is that some areas have encouraging preliminary evidence while many claims remain under-studied. Ayurveda is best used thoughtfully, alongside qualified medical guidance, especially in pregnancy, chronic illness, or when taking other medicines."
      }
    ],
